1
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:07,830
This is Software Engineering Radio, the podcast for professional developers on the web at SE-Radio.net.

2
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:11,870
SE Radio is brought to you by the IEEE Computer Society.

3
00:00:11,870 --> 00:00:19,070
and by IEEE Software Magazine online at computer.org/software.

4
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:23,870
Hello and welcome to Software Engineering Radio. This is your host Arun Ponnusamy.

5
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:30,510
Today I'm joined by Will Wilson, CEO and co-founder of Antithesis.

6
00:00:30,510 --> 00:00:36,510
Will started off in biotech and went on to work on distributed systems at FoundationDB, Apple, and Google.

7
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:44,550
And then he eventually went on to found Antithesis, an autonomous testing platform based on deterministic simulation testing.

8
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:47,510
Will, welcome to the show. Thank you so much for having me.

9
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:51,110
For our listeners who may not be familiar with the concept,

10
00:00:51,110 --> 00:00:55,150
could you share what is DST or deterministic simulation testing?

11
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:57,350
Yeah, sure.

12
00:00:57,350 --> 00:01:00,470
So, let's start with the way that people normally test software, right?

13
00:01:00,470 --> 00:01:02,590
So, the way they normally test software is first you write some code,

14
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:08,470
and then you try and think about what situations you want to cover with a test.

15
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:10,710
what situations you want to make sure are going to work in real life,

16
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:14,350
and then you write some tests to cover those situations, right?

17
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:17,630
And then you occasionally run those tests, maybe you do them on every CI build,

18
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:19,270
or maybe you do them on every PR.

19
00:01:19,270 --> 00:01:21,630
maybe you do them only when you're about to release your software.

20
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:27,150
but they cover those cases, and you hope that when you deploy to production,

21
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:29,110
everything is going to go okay.

22
00:01:29,110 --> 00:01:31,630
Now, let me ask you a question.

23
00:01:31,630 --> 00:01:34,870
Do you believe if all your tests pass, that everything is going to be okay?

24
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:36,830
I would like to think so.

25
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:38,750
But do you actually?

26
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:42,350
Uh well, if anything last 20 years have shown me, no.

27
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:44,951
Right, right. Exactly, so why is that?

28
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:48,150
Well, it's because we, I just said,

29
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:53,870
you're writing the tests that cover the cases that you thought were important to cover.

30
00:01:53,870 --> 00:01:59,270
But the things that are actually going to screw you in production are the situations. that you did not think of ahead of time.

31
00:01:59,270 --> 00:02:01,390
They're the things you did not think to cover, right?

32
00:02:01,390 --> 00:02:07,391
And so what that means is this conventional strategy to writing tests is very very good for catching. regressions, right?

33
00:02:07,391 --> 00:02:10,669
If there's something that I know my software does correctly.

34
00:02:10,669 --> 00:02:14,390
and I accidentally break it in a new version, if I have a test for it.

35
00:02:14,390 --> 00:02:16,230
that's a pretty good at catching that.

36
00:02:16,230 --> 00:02:21,630
But a test is very bad at finding out if my software actually works in the real world.

37
00:02:21,630 --> 00:02:27,030
in all the unanticipated or unexpected situations that the real world will throw at me.

38
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:31,990
So, deterministic simulation testing is a very different strategy,

39
00:02:31,990 --> 00:02:36,310
which tries to actually give us the confidence that if our test pass,

40
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:38,390
things will go well in the real world.

41
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:43,790
And the way it does that is basically by constructing a model of the real world,

42
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:46,190
a whole system simulation.

43
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:51,750
where random requests come in and ask your software to do things.

44
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:54,230
and tasks are going to run in random orders.

45
00:02:54,230 --> 00:02:58,230
and these requests are going to have slightly randomized parameters.

46
00:02:58,230 --> 00:02:59,949
And also the things that happen in the real world.

47
00:02:59,959 --> 00:03:04,030
that can go wrong are also going to happen, right?

48
00:03:04,030 --> 00:03:05,750
So, occasionally requests will fail.

49
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:07,750
Requests to downstream dependent systems will fail.

50
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:09,750
Network requests may get delayed or reordered.

51
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:14,110
Threads may randomly pause and run later.

52
00:03:14,110 --> 00:03:18,350
The garbage collector may just turn on and that whole node may slow down, right?

53
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:21,990
All the stuff that causes people to get paged in the middle of the night.

54
00:03:21,990 --> 00:03:25,310
and causes weird production behaviors that nobody can understand, we're going to do all that, too.

55
00:03:25,310 --> 00:03:29,870
And we're going to do it at a vastly accelerated rate.

56
00:03:29,870 --> 00:03:35,229
so that we cover many many years or centuries worth of real world badness.

57
00:03:35,229 --> 00:03:37,949
in just a few seconds or minutes in our tests.

58
00:03:37,949 --> 00:03:41,990
And if our software survives all that and things actually go okay,

59
00:03:41,990 --> 00:03:43,790
we're now going to have the confidence to actually deploy.

60
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:47,830
and to actually feel like things will be all right when we have it in the real world.

61
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:57,710
The final ingredient to this, the part that makes it deterministic is our model of the real world,

62
00:03:57,710 --> 00:04:03,070
unlike the real world, is perfectly deterministic.

63
00:04:03,070 --> 00:04:07,670
Meaning it's all driven from a random number generator that's under our control, right?

64
00:04:07,670 --> 00:04:13,190
So, this is very important because sometimes what happens is there's a particular.

65
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:19,110
Let's say that a packet takes 21 milliseconds to get from service A to service B.

66
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:22,270
And if it takes 19 milliseconds instead, something goes wrong, right?

67
00:04:22,270 --> 00:04:23,790
There's some distributed race condition.

68
00:04:23,790 --> 00:04:27,150
Something something loses and as a result there's some bug that a user can see.

69
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:30,710
You may have great difficulty replicating that situation again, right?

70
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:35,750
Maybe it only happened it only took that long because of some very rare set of circumstances.

71
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:40,830
But the determinism of DST means that any set of circumstances,

72
00:04:40,830 --> 00:04:45,110
no matter how rare, no matter how unusual, we can perfectly replicate.

73
00:04:45,110 --> 00:04:49,990
And so if we see a bug once, we can see it as many times as we want.

74
00:04:49,990 --> 00:04:51,750
and we can debug it with very very high efficiency.

75
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:56,230
How you actually achieve that is sort of the hard part.

76
00:04:56,230 --> 00:04:58,710
But once you have it, it's an incredible asset.

77
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:00,470
Awesome, thank you.

78
00:05:00,470 --> 00:05:05,390
Before I dive in, I would like to get some of the origin stories here.

79
00:05:05,390 --> 00:05:08,310
I hear it started off in FoundationDB. Could you share some of that background there?

80
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:12,550
Sure, yeah. So, this is sort of a fun story.

81
00:05:12,550 --> 00:05:19,230
Basically, actually even before FoundationDB, my co-founder Dave Scherer, before FoundationDB, had another company called Visual Sciences.

82
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:24,270
which you can think of as having made a very very early web analytics product.

83
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:31,791
that was backed by a very powerful analytic database that's sort of a very early version of Google. BigQuery, right?

84
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:36,870
And this would have been in the early 2000s. So, it was really very very ahead of its time.

85
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:46,510
So, when Dave was building that big analytic data store, he was really interested in using model-based techniques.

86
00:05:46,510 --> 00:05:52,510
to look for sort of long-tail performance pathologies and like strange sort of high latency corner cases.

87
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:59,831
And so he built this little like model based simulation of the database to see. if he could find these issues.

88
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:04,270
It's sort of like in some ways similar to formal methods if you're familiar with that,

89
00:06:04,270 --> 00:06:06,790
but he was using it to analyze performance rather than correctness.

90
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:12,470
The problem that Dave ran into was of course once he had this all working in the model.

91
00:06:12,480 --> 00:06:16,470
how do you know that your real code is behaving anything like the model, right?

92
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:18,630
And so that company got sold and.

93
00:06:18,630 --> 00:06:22,830
and that never went anywhere and but it stuck around in the back of his mind.

94
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:29,950
And so then when we were doing FoundationDB he had the same idea again this time to use.

95
00:06:29,950 --> 00:06:31,470
it to study correctness rather than performance.

96
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:39,711
But then he had the further idea what if we just made the actual program into the model, right?

97
00:06:39,711 --> 00:06:42,670
Then you would never have to worry.

98
00:06:42,670 --> 00:06:44,270
that oh I did some analysis on this model and it gave me this result.

99
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:49,790
but actually my real world code has a bug in it so it doesn't perfectly match that specification.

100
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:53,470
So the trouble is you know real world code has things like concurrency.

101
00:06:53,470 --> 00:06:55,510
and it has things like it reads the clock.

102
00:06:55,510 --> 00:06:58,830
and it gets random numbers from the operating system.

103
00:06:58,830 --> 00:07:02,510
and it it does all kinds of things that make it fundamentally non-deterministic.

104
00:07:02,510 --> 00:07:07,310
and make it so that if you run some battery of tests.

105
00:07:07,310 --> 00:07:09,830
or conduct some simulation the result might vary from time to time.

106
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:17,790
And so we set about writing FoundationDB in a very special way.

107
00:07:17,790 --> 00:07:20,350
so that it could be perfectly simulated.

108
00:07:20,350 --> 00:07:26,430
We did things like we used our own user space concurrency primitives.

109
00:07:26,430 --> 00:07:30,990
and scheduling so that the kernel was never deciding which task to run.

110
00:07:30,990 --> 00:07:33,750
It's like sort of similar to the Node.js concurrency model,

111
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:36,310
but we were doing that in C++ long beforehand.

112
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:42,430
We obviously had to mock out or stub out any form of network communication between nodes.

113
00:07:42,430 --> 00:07:46,830
and handle that in process. We had a fake clock and a fake random number generator.

114
00:07:46,830 --> 00:07:51,710
that got accessed instead of the ones provided by the OS.

115
00:07:51,710 --> 00:07:53,350
And as we sort of knocked away each of these forms of randomness.

116
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:57,870
and each of these forms of non-determinism, the simulation became more controllable.

117
00:07:57,880 --> 00:08:00,110
until we eventually got to a point.

118
00:08:00,110 --> 00:08:04,550
where it really was like you could really just run a program.

119
00:08:04,550 --> 00:08:08,030
and it would simulate an entire interacting FoundationDB cluster.

120
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:15,270
with possibly dozens or hundreds of simulated nodes speaking over simulated networks.

121
00:08:15,270 --> 00:08:22,030
with, you know, randomly introduced networking delays and congestion behavior and whatever else you want.

122
00:08:22,030 --> 00:08:27,830
And this was just such an insanely productive mode in which to work.

123
00:08:27,830 --> 00:08:34,029
Like people who are familiar with FoundationDB these days mostly know it for its reputation,

124
00:08:34,029 --> 00:08:38,469
which is being one of the highest quality databases ever made.

125
00:08:38,469 --> 00:08:40,469
never losing your data, never doing anything wrong.

126
00:08:40,469 --> 00:08:42,070
And it's true, we got that.

127
00:08:42,070 --> 00:08:44,790
And we got that because of this technique.

128
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:46,750
But the thing that made almost an even bigger difference was.

129
00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:50,230
that we were able to get that with a very, very small team.

130
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:58,110
Because what this technique enables is like this incredible form of pair programming with the computer.

131
00:08:58,120 --> 00:09:02,150
where you write some code and then you run some simulations and you say,

132
00:09:02,150 --> 00:09:04,510
"Did I introduce any bugs?"

133
00:09:04,510 --> 00:09:09,030
And, you know, if the answer is yes, then like you just try again.

134
00:09:09,030 --> 00:09:12,110
It's like having an oracle or a genie that can tell you if your code is correct, right?

135
00:09:12,110 --> 00:09:16,710
It's like this incredible, incredible accelerator for an entire team.

136
00:09:16,710 --> 00:09:18,430
And that was what made us really excited about this technique.

137
00:09:18,440 --> 00:09:23,310
and I think this is why so many others have started adopting it. and picking it up as well.

138
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:26,630
Interesting. That reminds me as you go deeper, right?

139
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:30,550
I mean to really simulation, you need a model.

140
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:35,430
So, how do you avoid the paradox of hey look, I don't know what I don't know and I build this model?

141
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:40,030
Yeah, that is a really, really good question.

142
00:09:40,030 --> 00:09:43,830
And basically, the answer is you have to err a little bit on the side of your model.

143
00:09:43,830 --> 00:09:45,590
being worse than reality, rather than better than reality, right?

144
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:47,950
Could you give an example of that, what worse looks like?

145
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:51,270
Yeah, sure.

146
00:09:51,270 --> 00:09:56,510
So, your network in your data center is occasionally going to have periods of being down.

147
00:09:56,510 --> 00:09:59,750
and it's going to occasionally have periods where it's a little bit slow.

148
00:09:59,750 --> 00:10:03,030
and it's occasionally going to have periods where it's like working great.

149
00:10:03,030 --> 00:10:11,510
And these periods in your network are probably totally uncorrelated with what your program is doing, right?

150
00:10:11,510 --> 00:10:13,870
And so, suppose that there's some bug,

151
00:10:13,870 --> 00:10:18,590
and the bug only happens if your program is in the middle of some particular operation, right?

152
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:25,830
Like you you're doing some I don't know, you're doing some transaction on your database.

153
00:10:25,830 --> 00:10:29,390
and at exactly that moment, the network does something a little bit wrong, right?

154
00:10:29,390 --> 00:10:31,070
Or it's a little bit slower than you expect, or whatever.

155
00:10:31,070 --> 00:10:34,510
And only if these two things happen at the same time, this bug happens.

156
00:10:34,510 --> 00:10:36,830
And let's say the bug is really, really bad.

157
00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:43,350
In order to make that bug happen very reliably and very reproducibly in our tests,

158
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:48,990
we don't want to make the network completely uncorrelated from what your program is doing.

159
00:10:48,990 --> 00:10:54,110
We want to make it basically as malicious as it can be, right?

160
00:10:54,110 --> 00:10:56,829
We want to try making the network bad at each different sort of moment.

161
00:10:56,839 --> 00:10:59,310
that your program could be doing something.

162
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:02,910
and we want to like artificially increase the probability.

163
00:11:02,910 --> 00:11:06,870
that this network badness happens at exactly the right spot to trigger that bug.

164
00:11:06,870 --> 00:11:11,430
Because that way you see it in your test like in a few seconds or a few minutes,

165
00:11:11,430 --> 00:11:13,430
rather than having to wait for days or weeks.

166
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:16,910
which you would have to do if it were happening in production.

167
00:11:16,910 --> 00:11:23,750
And so, that's kind of an example of where if you make the simulation more hostile than reality.

168
00:11:23,750 --> 00:11:29,230
it can catch stuff quicker than it would happen in the real world.

169
00:11:29,230 --> 00:11:31,310
and also ensures that you don't miss things that That happen in the real world.

170
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:33,830
Mhm.

171
00:11:33,830 --> 00:11:38,190
So, what would be a good I suppose evaluation.

172
00:11:38,190 --> 00:11:40,750
or identification function to know when or what a bad network is.

173
00:11:40,750 --> 00:11:45,150
For example, a network loss of 10% may be really good in some applications,

174
00:11:45,150 --> 00:11:47,230
but really bad in some. So, how do you decide?

175
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:50,470
That's totally true.

176
00:11:50,470 --> 00:11:53,110
And furthermore, there can be a very dangerous pattern,

177
00:11:53,120 --> 00:11:59,710
where if you basically make these parameters a like a little bit wrong, right?

178
00:11:59,710 --> 00:12:03,070
You could accidentally get yourself in a situation where the simulation isn't really testing anything.

179
00:12:03,080 --> 00:12:05,030
Right?

180
00:12:05,030 --> 00:12:08,390
Consider the corner case where we just make it so the network never transports anything at all.

181
00:12:08,390 --> 00:12:11,830
Now, your program never does anything. And then all your tests are green.

182
00:12:11,830 --> 00:12:14,470
because we didn't find any bugs because your program didn't do anything.

183
00:12:14,470 --> 00:12:16,670
That would be a bad outcome, right?

184
00:12:16,680 --> 00:12:20,750
And so, there's a few techniques that we can use to to avoid this.

185
00:12:20,750 --> 00:12:26,230
and to make the simulation really good and really well-tuned instead.

186
00:12:26,230 --> 00:12:30,230
One is just let's look at things like code coverage,

187
00:12:30,230 --> 00:12:34,550
or the more sophisticated version of this is like behavioral coverage, right?

188
00:12:34,550 --> 00:12:38,430
If you have certain corner cases in your code.

189
00:12:38,430 --> 00:12:40,230
that you know are interesting and are important to cover,

190
00:12:40,230 --> 00:12:44,630
you should just log a little message when that situation happens.

191
00:12:44,630 --> 00:12:46,950
And then at the end of a test run,

192
00:12:46,950 --> 00:12:50,270
we should make an assertion that we're able to see all those messages, right?

193
00:12:50,270 --> 00:12:52,550
That we basically got at some point to every one of those situations.

194
00:12:52,560 --> 00:12:58,390
And the generalization of that is something that we call sometimes assertions.

195
00:12:58,390 --> 00:13:00,470
They're like the dual or the opposite of normal assertions, right?

196
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:05,470
Rather than asserting that something is always or never happening, we're asserting that it sometimes happens.

197
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:10,990
because that's an important indication about whether our test is working effectively.

198
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,310
Is that in a probabilistic model or is it truly sometimes?

199
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:17,870
Right, right. So, it's like at least one, right?

200
00:13:17,870 --> 00:13:19,990
We're saying that if I run my program a thousand times,

201
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,870
I want to guarantee that I see this behavior at least once.

202
00:13:23,870 --> 00:13:26,150
That is a property of my testing system, right?

203
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:30,070
It's like I should be able to make the situation occur.

204
00:13:30,070 --> 00:13:33,390
Like the ultimate guarantor though is just like are you still seeing bugs in production?

205
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:38,510
Basically, I think people regardless of whether doing DST or not,

206
00:13:38,510 --> 00:13:42,270
if you ever see a bug in production that is actually two bugs.

207
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:46,790
It's a bug in your program and it's also a bug in your tests.

208
00:13:46,790 --> 00:13:48,390
and you should maybe not solve them in that order.

209
00:13:48,390 --> 00:13:50,190
Maybe you should solve the bug in your test first.

210
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:52,030
Who knows what else it will turn up.

211
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:55,790
And this is like a very This is a a practice.

212
00:13:55,790 --> 00:13:58,910
that requires a little bit of discipline to get into,

213
00:13:58,910 --> 00:14:01,750
but once you do, it's it's so incredibly powerful.

214
00:14:01,750 --> 00:14:10,230
Just like understand why it is that you didn't see that bug the minute it was introduced.

215
00:14:10,230 --> 00:14:15,990
And often it will point to an entire area of your of your software.

216
00:14:15,990 --> 00:14:18,470
or of your organization or whatever that is not working correctly.

217
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:21,110
Mhm.

218
00:14:21,110 --> 00:14:25,030
So it it sounded like there was a bootstrap phase here for a team to kind of onboard.

219
00:14:25,030 --> 00:14:28,630
Let's let's say I'm writing a game, right? A multiplayer game running on the internet.

220
00:14:28,630 --> 00:14:30,270
It's probably as low level as it gets, right?

221
00:14:30,280 --> 00:14:33,910
What would the bootstrap phase for me be to adopt this?

222
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:38,190
Right, so this gets a little bit into like sort of what our company does.

223
00:14:38,200 --> 00:14:44,910
Basically, up until we came around, this technique was very very powerful,

224
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:48,070
but it was a giant pain to adopt.

225
00:14:48,070 --> 00:14:52,750
because it required you to do things like mock out your dependencies.

226
00:14:52,750 --> 00:14:57,390
and somehow take control of like concurrency and scheduling within your program.

227
00:14:57,390 --> 00:14:59,270
simulate all of your network connections, right?

228
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:04,630
There there's sort of a lot that had to be done.

229
00:15:04,630 --> 00:15:06,870
And there were applications and use cases.

230
00:15:06,870 --> 00:15:13,270
where reliability and correctness was so important that people would do all these things.

231
00:15:13,270 --> 00:15:16,230
Like I I know of actually quite a large number of people.

232
00:15:16,230 --> 00:15:18,310
now cuz they they come talk to me, right?

233
00:15:18,310 --> 00:15:23,510
Who actually started doing this in the years after FoundationDB got acquired by Apple.

234
00:15:23,510 --> 00:15:28,270
The team at Dropbox that worked on their synchronization technology built their own DST.

235
00:15:28,270 --> 00:15:32,070
Tiger Beetle is a very well-known financial transactions database.

236
00:15:32,070 --> 00:15:34,191
They built their own DST. It's It's quite good.

237
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:38,830
You know, a number of people actually in the finance.

238
00:15:38,830 --> 00:15:43,510
or fintech or or crypto space built their own DST and did very good job at it.

239
00:15:43,510 --> 00:15:47,310
because they really wanted to catch any kind of problem before it hit prod.

240
00:15:47,310 --> 00:15:49,190
because it could result in losing tons of money.

241
00:15:49,190 --> 00:15:55,550
And obviously other database vendors were also doing this as were a number of teams.

242
00:15:55,550 --> 00:15:59,030
that got influenced by what we had done at FoundationDB. And by the way, after I went to work at Google,

243
00:15:59,030 --> 00:16:02,910
I learned that there were teams inside Google.

244
00:16:02,910 --> 00:16:05,190
that had done this and just never told anybody about it.

245
00:16:05,190 --> 00:16:09,310
So, I think this is a technique that's been independently reinvented a few times.

246
00:16:09,320 --> 00:16:17,230
But, it remained a thing that only very very committed people.

247
00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:22,510
who really felt like their program had to be perfectly reliable would do.

248
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:25,070
because the adoption costs were high.

249
00:16:25,080 --> 00:16:35,350
So, our new company, Antithesis, tries to drastically reduce the adoption cost for this style of testing.

250
00:16:35,350 --> 00:16:39,590
by basically doing like solving two of the problems that you and I have already discussed.

251
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:46,750
One is how do you take an arbitrary collection of software and make it deterministic, right?

252
00:16:46,750 --> 00:16:50,790
And prior to to us being around, that took a lot of work.

253
00:16:50,790 --> 00:16:54,790
And what we've done is write a hypervisor, which is deterministic.

254
00:16:54,790 --> 00:16:59,070
And you can take an arbitrary collection of software and just put it in the hypervisor,

255
00:16:59,070 --> 00:17:00,630
and now it's deterministic, too.

256
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:05,189
And so, that already on its own makes this much easier.

257
00:17:05,199 --> 00:17:10,550
The second problem is the one you touched on, which is what's the right network drop rate, right?

258
00:17:10,550 --> 00:17:14,150
What is the right way of tuning all these parameters of the simulation to find all the bugs.

259
00:17:14,160 --> 00:17:16,069
and to find those quickly as possible.

260
00:17:16,079 --> 00:17:23,390
This is like a very hard optimization machine learning science problem.

261
00:17:23,390 --> 00:17:27,069
And it's sort of unreasonable in my opinion for every team on earth to try.

262
00:17:27,069 --> 00:17:30,550
and solve this independently.

263
00:17:30,550 --> 00:17:33,390
And so we have also just done a huge amount of research.

264
00:17:33,390 --> 00:17:39,790
and a huge amount of development to make this simulation like self-tuning.

265
00:17:39,790 --> 00:17:47,670
and to make it very intelligent and very good at finding the correct parameters and the correct settings. So you just don't even have to think about that.

266
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:53,351
And so the goal here is to make it much much much easier for people to adopt this. stuff.

267
00:17:53,351 --> 00:17:59,590
which hopefully just makes them a lot faster at writing software and also produce much higher quality software.

268
00:17:59,600 --> 00:18:02,510
I want to quickly touch back on a point you mentioned about the cost, right?

269
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:09,150
What would the cost for these organizations to adopt DST in their own setting engineering wise, financially?

270
00:18:09,160 --> 00:18:13,670
Yeah, I think if you don't have a solution like antithesis,

271
00:18:13,670 --> 00:18:18,751
then the the question is really dominated by your dependencies. Right?

272
00:18:18,751 --> 00:18:23,550
Because the hard thing about DST is that unless you have a hypervisor,

273
00:18:23,550 --> 00:18:27,710
you need to somehow make it so all your dependencies are deterministic too.

274
00:18:27,720 --> 00:18:35,430
And that like really does just hugely limit what you can do.

275
00:18:35,430 --> 00:18:37,030
Most software in the world is not deterministic, right?

276
00:18:37,030 --> 00:18:43,270
It's got concurrency, it's got threads, it it checks timers, it does all kinds of stuff.

277
00:18:43,270 --> 00:18:48,510
When we were developing FoundationDB, we were.

278
00:18:48,510 --> 00:18:53,350
so influenced by this DST style of development that we actually started removing our dependencies.

279
00:18:53,350 --> 00:18:56,430
to make it easier to test in this way.

280
00:18:56,430 --> 00:19:03,550
So very early versions of our database were using Apache Zookeeper for coordination for instance.

281
00:19:03,550 --> 00:19:05,230
And that just became a thing that we could no longer accept.

282
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:07,670
because it made it harder to simulate.

283
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:15,431
And so we deleted Zookeeper and wrote our own Paxos implementation that could be simulated. and that made sense for us.

284
00:19:15,440 --> 00:19:19,950
But that's a very, very hard thing to ask somebody to do.

285
00:19:19,950 --> 00:19:21,830
if they're just starting to adopt this technology.

286
00:19:21,830 --> 00:19:24,070
which is one reason that I think.

287
00:19:24,070 --> 00:19:27,830
that thus far it's only been it's only happened in relatively specialized places.

288
00:19:27,840 --> 00:19:32,270
It's almost like asking them to re-reinvent or re-implement what you what you guys are doing.

289
00:19:32,280 --> 00:19:34,670
Yeah, that's right.

290
00:19:34,670 --> 00:19:37,790
It's it's just it's just a very high barrier to entry and that's what we're trying to fix.

291
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:41,270
Fair enough. That brings me back to the hypervisor that you have mentioned.

292
00:19:41,270 --> 00:19:43,110
I mean, can you tell me more about that?

293
00:19:43,120 --> 00:19:44,950
Sure.

294
00:19:44,950 --> 00:19:48,950
So we started from the question of how could we make DST easy to adopt?

295
00:19:48,950 --> 00:19:51,070
And it's like, well, we need to make it.

296
00:19:51,070 --> 00:19:54,550
so that everybody can make their program deterministic super easily.

297
00:19:54,550 --> 00:19:57,750
And then it's like, well you know,

298
00:19:57,750 --> 00:20:03,030
if your program is written in Java, you could try and make a deterministic JVM. And if your program's written in C,

299
00:20:03,030 --> 00:20:07,631
I guess you could try and make a deterministic Linux user space, right? Actually, Facebook did that.

300
00:20:07,631 --> 00:20:09,150
That project's called Hermit.

301
00:20:09,160 --> 00:20:12,790
But we're going to have customers who use every language.

302
00:20:12,790 --> 00:20:15,350
So we can't just like play whack-a-mole here doing all these things.

303
00:20:15,360 --> 00:20:21,870
And then it's like, well, so much of the value is being able to test microservices.

304
00:20:21,870 --> 00:20:24,190
where service A is talking to service B over a network.

305
00:20:24,190 --> 00:20:26,270
But now we need to make that networking deterministic.

306
00:20:26,270 --> 00:20:28,670
How are we going to do that?

307
00:20:28,670 --> 00:20:30,710
Like, oh wow, we need some crazy networking library that we can drop in,

308
00:20:30,720 --> 00:20:35,311
but everybody does networking differently. How do we?

309
00:20:35,311 --> 00:20:39,230
Right, like so very, very quickly you get to like,

310
00:20:39,230 --> 00:20:43,190
well I guess we should try and get under all of these things.

311
00:20:43,190 --> 00:20:45,190
and make a deterministic operating system.

312
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:46,950
But then you hit another problem.

313
00:20:46,950 --> 00:20:50,030
Well, first of all, not everybody in the world uses Linux.

314
00:20:50,030 --> 00:20:54,230
and we would love to be able to have customers on Windows someday and and so on.

315
00:20:54,230 --> 00:21:01,790
But also the interface between the Linux kernel and everything running on top of it is really complicated.

316
00:21:01,790 --> 00:21:03,751
And it's always changing. Right?

317
00:21:03,760 --> 00:21:12,710
And so maintaining a perfect deterministic Linux as a small company just seemed like a very tall order.

318
00:21:12,720 --> 00:21:14,670
And then we were like, you know what?

319
00:21:14,680 --> 00:21:21,110
You know what is a very simple interface that has not changed in 40 50 years?

320
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:24,350
The x86 CPU instructions.

321
00:21:24,350 --> 00:21:30,830
Like look, so let's just get under the operating system and let's let's emulate a deterministic computer.

322
00:21:30,840 --> 00:21:35,470
Where no matter what the operating system does,

323
00:21:35,470 --> 00:21:37,790
no matter what interrupts it sets, no matter how it,

324
00:21:37,790 --> 00:21:42,230
you know, is managing memory or doing anything from the outside.

325
00:21:42,230 --> 00:21:46,510
or rather from the inside, it it's like living in a perfect isolated hermetic deterministic universe.

326
00:21:46,520 --> 00:21:49,350
and it it can't actually cause any non-determinism.

327
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:53,590
And then once you build that it's like so easy to adopt.

328
00:21:53,590 --> 00:21:57,070
Now people don't have to change their software at all.

329
00:21:57,070 --> 00:22:03,270
They just give it to us, we run it inside the magic box, and it's perfectly deterministic. So it's it's really very very powerful.

330
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:07,630
How does this overcome the I mean this box is still in isolation.

331
00:22:07,630 --> 00:22:13,750
or is external to your TCP stack itself or or the network layer itself? So how do you reconcile that?

332
00:22:13,760 --> 00:22:15,910
Right.

333
00:22:15,910 --> 00:22:20,750
So basically what we do is we take our customers' multiple microservices, right?

334
00:22:20,750 --> 00:22:24,790
Representing a bunch of different physical nodes in their in their compute back end.

335
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:26,790
Plus whatever their dependencies are.

336
00:22:26,800 --> 00:22:31,270
Maybe they have a database or maybe they've got some API server or whatever.

337
00:22:31,280 --> 00:22:38,631
And we take them all and we put them inside the same guest in operating system environment, which is Linux.

338
00:22:38,631 --> 00:22:44,671
And then we connect them over TCP locally inside the VM. Right?

339
00:22:44,671 --> 00:22:50,751
So they can speak over network connections between different Docker containers running all in the same version of. Linux.

340
00:22:50,760 --> 00:22:55,470
And then we can actually reach in and interfere with those network connections, right?

341
00:22:55,470 --> 00:22:59,990
We can slow the packets, we can drop packets, we can we can do whatever.

342
00:22:59,990 --> 00:23:03,590
We can create simulated partitions, we can and we can correlate all this with the code that's running,

343
00:23:03,600 --> 00:23:09,390
right? The thing I told you before. Because we see what code is running cuz we control the operating system.

344
00:23:09,390 --> 00:23:12,270
And so it's actually it's actually a very complete solution.

345
00:23:12,280 --> 00:23:14,510
It works very well.

346
00:23:14,510 --> 00:23:16,830
The one thing that's a little bit of friction is.

347
00:23:16,830 --> 00:23:21,910
if you are highly dependent on some third-party web service.

348
00:23:21,910 --> 00:23:24,670
that you cannot put in a container and have a hard time mocking.

349
00:23:24,680 --> 00:23:26,830
That is going to cause a problem.

350
00:23:26,830 --> 00:23:30,430
So we try and reduce that friction for our customers also by writing very good,

351
00:23:30,440 --> 00:23:35,630
very high-quality mocks for a lot of common web services.

352
00:23:35,630 --> 00:23:38,510
So for example, we have a whole fake AWS.

353
00:23:38,510 --> 00:23:40,710
that we can like run inside the simulation with you.

354
00:23:40,710 --> 00:23:44,790
And if your program uses AWS, it just runs in there.

355
00:23:44,790 --> 00:23:46,470
and uses the fake one and can't even tell the difference.

356
00:23:46,470 --> 00:23:50,110
We actually test our own stuff that way cuz we're very heavy AWS users.

357
00:23:50,120 --> 00:23:53,710
We can do the same for Snowflake and GCP and Stripe and you know, whatever.

358
00:23:53,710 --> 00:23:55,670
We can write a good mock for it.

359
00:23:55,670 --> 00:24:02,791
And that's that's nice because basically just a few of those covers the vast majority of APIs. that people use.

360
00:24:02,800 --> 00:24:06,990
And if there's a long tail, you know, I think we'll we'll get to it someday.

361
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:12,030
The other thing that the hypervisor gives us,

362
00:24:12,030 --> 00:24:15,910
which is very, very powerful, is extremely efficient.

363
00:24:15,910 --> 00:24:21,630
and rapid snapshotting and restore of the whole state of the guest operating system.

364
00:24:21,640 --> 00:24:23,510
Right?

365
00:24:23,510 --> 00:24:25,670
So if you use a VMware hypervisor,

366
00:24:25,670 --> 00:24:29,510
for instance, you can like snapshot that VM and then you can reload it, right?

367
00:24:29,510 --> 00:24:31,350
And that that takes a little bit of time, but you can do it.

368
00:24:31,350 --> 00:24:38,030
Our hypervisor is optimized to perform that operation incredibly quickly and efficiently.

369
00:24:38,040 --> 00:24:47,510
More than that even, when it's running multiple VMs simultaneously on the same computer.

370
00:24:47,510 --> 00:24:55,351
those VMs can actually address the same physical memory if their local version of that memory hasn't changed. Right?

371
00:24:55,351 --> 00:25:02,510
So we're basically doing copy-on-write on all of the memory of the guest operating system.

372
00:25:02,510 --> 00:25:04,510
So, now if we run 100 VMs in parallel on the same machine,

373
00:25:04,510 --> 00:25:06,510
they're not using 100 times the memory.

374
00:25:06,510 --> 00:25:10,750
We're basically like deduplicating all of the memory between all the different VMs,

375
00:25:10,760 --> 00:25:14,350
which is a thing that I don't know anybody else's hypervisor can do that.

376
00:25:14,360 --> 00:25:15,950
I'm sure there is one somewhere.

377
00:25:15,950 --> 00:25:19,550
But, what it means is that we can now test your stuff really efficiently, right?

378
00:25:19,560 --> 00:25:25,310
Cuz we can use huge amounts of parallelism without drastically increasing the memory requirements.

379
00:25:25,320 --> 00:25:28,870
which just at the end of the day means that we can find bugs faster,

380
00:25:28,870 --> 00:25:30,990
and we can find rare bugs faster.

381
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:35,710
It's interesting because the implication of this means that a,

382
00:25:35,710 --> 00:25:38,630
you kind of running the entire customer world in a single sandbox.

383
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:43,790
And you can also do things like simulate connection between A.

384
00:25:43,790 --> 00:25:47,470
and B if it was in the same zone or same region or even across regions.

385
00:25:47,480 --> 00:25:53,070
Because you want to know that, hey, look, this is for DR and for various kinds of uh a fault tolerance, right?

386
00:25:53,080 --> 00:25:59,350
That's exactly right. All that we have to do is really change the latency, right? On the virtual connection. Or we And error rates.

387
00:25:59,360 --> 00:26:02,430
And error rates, that's right. And also, you know, correlated failures, right?

388
00:26:02,440 --> 00:26:08,390
We'll make things in the same DR region fail together more often, but not across regions.

389
00:26:08,390 --> 00:26:11,430
And people use us to test DR all the time.

390
00:26:11,430 --> 00:26:14,270
People also love using us to test live upgrades.

391
00:26:14,270 --> 00:26:16,630
Cuz that's a very scary situation for many teams.

392
00:26:16,630 --> 00:26:20,550
You have some big service deployed, and you have customers who are using it, right?

393
00:26:20,550 --> 00:26:24,590
And you want to incrementally upgrade your cluster.

394
00:26:24,590 --> 00:26:26,390
And while the upgrade is rolling out,

395
00:26:26,390 --> 00:26:31,430
you're going to have servers with both the old version and the new version serving requests simultaneously.

396
00:26:31,440 --> 00:26:33,470
Does that all work?

397
00:26:33,470 --> 00:26:35,670
It can be very hard to test that.

398
00:26:35,670 --> 00:26:37,470
But, it's quite easy to test it with our environment.

399
00:26:37,480 --> 00:26:39,830
Interesting.

400
00:26:39,830 --> 00:26:41,870
So, two things here from a quote-unquote cost perspective.

401
00:26:41,880 --> 00:26:46,230
What is the biggest installation that you run so far on this?

402
00:26:46,230 --> 00:26:50,190
Like, you know, how many services, how many instances, RAM, cores?

403
00:26:50,200 --> 00:26:53,751
Yeah. So, the biggest one that I know of.

404
00:26:53,760 --> 00:26:58,790
So we have a customer WarpStream, which was recently acquired by Confluent.

405
00:26:58,800 --> 00:27:03,150
Yeah, so I think they may be our largest one.

406
00:27:03,150 --> 00:27:08,510
So So they develop like a Kafka replacement with blob storage on S3, right?

407
00:27:08,510 --> 00:27:12,070
And so we started out just testing their Kafka replacement and and making sure that that was good.

408
00:27:12,070 --> 00:27:15,390
and the things that were really durable were really durable and and so on.

409
00:27:15,400 --> 00:27:24,271
And then what happened was they added all the rest of their SaaS architecture into the simulation as. well.

410
00:27:24,271 --> 00:27:30,830
So the same simulation is not just testing their Kafka and their operations with S3 and so on.

411
00:27:30,830 --> 00:27:32,750
It's also testing like user sign-ups and like,

412
00:27:32,750 --> 00:27:38,110
you know, people using their admin APIs to like create clusters and it's like totally crazy.

413
00:27:38,110 --> 00:27:43,150
It is actually far beyond what I've ever done with DST, but it works.

414
00:27:43,160 --> 00:27:46,590
How big was it? I would have to go look it up. I do not know the answer off the top of my head.

415
00:27:46,600 --> 00:27:50,830
But I But I think there were like a many many many different services involved.

416
00:27:50,840 --> 00:27:53,951
Okay. Fair enough.

417
00:27:53,951 --> 00:27:57,710
In terms of their own testing phase, typically how long do customers run this for?

418
00:27:57,710 --> 00:27:59,951
Is it a parallel thing? Is it a ongoing thing?

419
00:27:59,951 --> 00:28:01,550
What is the workflow for them?

420
00:28:01,560 --> 00:28:03,430
Yeah, that's a great question.

421
00:28:03,430 --> 00:28:09,510
So I think there's two main ways that people do this.

422
00:28:09,510 --> 00:28:11,630
And one of our long-term product goals,

423
00:28:11,630 --> 00:28:14,630
actually not even long-term, one of our relatively short-term.

424
00:28:14,630 --> 00:28:16,910
now product goals is to bridge these two ways of using it.

425
00:28:16,910 --> 00:28:24,110
So the first way people use this is sort of offline as a very intensive check phase,

426
00:28:24,120 --> 00:28:27,270
maybe overnight, right?

427
00:28:27,270 --> 00:28:30,390
Or maybe like they do tons and tons of testing right before release,

428
00:28:30,390 --> 00:28:32,710
before rolling out a release, right?

429
00:28:32,710 --> 00:28:34,990
So the idea here is like you spend all day writing code.

430
00:28:34,990 --> 00:28:38,670
and then whatever the last version of your code is, the default branch.

431
00:28:38,670 --> 00:28:42,550
you send that to us when people leave work. and then we test it all night.

432
00:28:42,550 --> 00:28:47,631
and then in the morning you come in and you see what your results are. And that can be cool.

433
00:28:47,640 --> 00:28:50,910
It does mean that we can find pretty rare,

434
00:28:50,910 --> 00:28:53,070
pretty hard-to-find bugs because we're testing for 12 hours or whatever.

435
00:28:53,080 --> 00:28:57,790
And but they're still fresh in your head and it's probably code that you changed yesterday,

436
00:28:57,790 --> 00:29:00,310
so it's still high productivity to work on those problems.

437
00:29:00,320 --> 00:29:05,470
The other way of using it, which is much more similar to the way.

438
00:29:05,470 --> 00:29:09,790
that we used it at FoundationDB, well, I guess we actually did both there.

439
00:29:09,790 --> 00:29:12,310
But But the other way of using it is in the loop, right?

440
00:29:12,310 --> 00:29:14,470
Like while you're coding.

441
00:29:14,470 --> 00:29:17,190
So, you write some code, you make some change, you kick off a test.

442
00:29:17,200 --> 00:29:20,390
And hopefully, if you want to do it this way,

443
00:29:20,390 --> 00:29:23,430
you need to make it so that your tests can run very fast.

444
00:29:23,430 --> 00:29:25,910
or can give you good information very quickly.

445
00:29:25,910 --> 00:29:29,830
So that hopefully, like within 15 minutes or maybe an hour at most,

446
00:29:29,830 --> 00:29:35,350
you get some answer that's like was there anything obviously broken this, right?

447
00:29:35,350 --> 00:29:37,710
And that can be a very, very powerful iteration loop.

448
00:29:37,720 --> 00:29:42,150
I think the ideal is to have both.

