00:31:50,351 --> 00:31:52,790
We're also going to use this for this kind of correlation stuff,

481
00:31:52,800 --> 00:31:58,270
for saying, "Oh, we're in like code block A.

482
00:31:58,270 --> 00:32:02,151
We've never tried pausing a thread while we're in code block A before. Let's do that now."

483
00:32:02,151 --> 00:32:06,150
Or like, "Oh, this microservice is running function X,

484
00:32:06,160 --> 00:32:10,150
and simultaneously this other one over here is also running function X.

485
00:32:10,150 --> 00:32:16,030
Let's interrupt the network connection between them right now and see what happens, right?"

486
00:32:16,030 --> 00:32:17,870
And we're going to do that very much in parallel.

487
00:32:17,870 --> 00:32:23,190
And then, the fact that the hypervisor lets us cheaply snapshot.

488
00:32:23,190 --> 00:32:28,030
and restore stuff means that we can go back to situations that we found interesting.

489
00:32:28,030 --> 00:32:30,390
and try exploring more from that point.

490
00:32:30,390 --> 00:32:33,310
And that basically lets us amplify the probability of very,

491
00:32:33,310 --> 00:32:40,510
very rare events to find really, really hard to discover bugs.

492
00:32:40,510 --> 00:32:44,910
I'll give you a concrete example because that was a little bit vague.

493
00:32:44,910 --> 00:32:49,270
Suppose you have a bug and in order for this bug to happen you need to be in.

494
00:32:49,270 --> 00:32:51,110
the middle of a transaction rollback.

495
00:32:51,120 --> 00:32:54,030
and there needs to be some network problem.

496
00:32:54,040 --> 00:33:00,790
and right at that moment something has to trigger a leader election in your cluster or something.

497
00:33:00,800 --> 00:33:07,230
If each of those events has a one in 1,000 chance of happening at any given moment,

498
00:33:07,230 --> 00:33:09,870
that's like a one in a billion bug, right?

499
00:33:09,870 --> 00:33:11,550
That's like very, very hard for a test to find normally.

500
00:33:11,560 --> 00:33:16,150
But if you're running it in production and you're running on thousands of nodes and whatever,

501
00:33:16,150 --> 00:33:17,950
like your customers will totally hit that bug.

502
00:33:17,950 --> 00:33:22,470
So this is like a classic example of a bug that normal testing would never,

503
00:33:22,470 --> 00:33:24,350
ever find and that would cause you to get in a page.

504
00:33:24,350 --> 00:33:26,430
And so, but what we can do.

505
00:33:26,430 --> 00:33:32,310
because we can see what code is running is when we hit that one in 1,000 transaction rollback.

506
00:33:32,320 --> 00:33:35,150
we're going to say, "Aha, we made a transaction rollback.

507
00:33:35,150 --> 00:33:37,510
We've never seen that before."

508
00:33:37,510 --> 00:33:40,270
Let's do more testing from this point onwards.

509
00:33:40,270 --> 00:33:42,510
And each of the tries we do from that point,

510
00:33:42,510 --> 00:33:45,030
if it doesn't hit the next event of interest.

511
00:33:45,040 --> 00:33:51,470
we can just reset to the transaction rollback and try more stuff from there, right?

512
00:33:51,470 --> 00:33:53,750
It's like if you're playing a computer game.

513
00:33:53,750 --> 00:33:57,630
and you can save your game anytime you want it's easier to make progress.

514
00:33:57,630 --> 00:33:59,790
because you can always just reload your game.

515
00:33:59,790 --> 00:34:01,470
and and go to the point where things were going well.

516
00:34:01,480 --> 00:34:04,990
And so basically what this means is.

517
00:34:04,990 --> 00:34:11,669
that for these bugs that require multiple different rare things to happen in sequence.

518
00:34:11,669 --> 00:34:14,790
we can find them at a vastly accelerated rate compared to traditional techniques.

519
00:34:14,800 --> 00:34:17,869
You know, I love the example of the saving the game and going back again.

520
00:34:17,869 --> 00:34:21,149
I spent my '90s doing that in all those old games.

