00:34:25,990 --> 00:34:33,030
So we believe that a good testing system should be able to beat a computer game in the. interest of testing it. And indeed, if you go to our website,

523
00:34:33,030 --> 00:34:37,710
we have many videos of Antithesis playing classic computer games and beating them.

524
00:34:37,720 --> 00:34:41,310
I saw the uh Mario example at S Recon last year.

525
00:34:41,320 --> 00:34:43,310
So, back to the example, right?

526
00:34:43,310 --> 00:34:48,470
So, what what you said was when you hit that one in a thousand transaction rollback scenario,

527
00:34:48,480 --> 00:34:50,389
you still need that scenario to be hit.

528
00:34:50,399 --> 00:34:53,389
Is there a way you can kind of say like this block of code.

529
00:34:53,389 --> 00:34:56,990
actually through some sequence of code paths could get hit.

530
00:34:56,990 --> 00:34:59,950
and can you force that to happen proactively instead of waiting for it?

531
00:34:59,960 --> 00:35:03,630
So, that's a great question.

532
00:35:03,630 --> 00:35:07,390
We try very hard to make it so that everything gets hit,

533
00:35:07,390 --> 00:35:09,750
and there's a number of techniques that we use to do that, right?

534
00:35:09,750 --> 00:35:15,430
One is a very, I think very underused technique called swarm testing,

535
00:35:15,430 --> 00:35:19,430
which is applicable to people who are not even doing DST.

536
00:35:19,430 --> 00:35:21,391
Just everybody needs to know about this technique. Okay.

537
00:35:21,400 --> 00:35:26,630
And so, the the the basic idea of swarm testing is you sometimes should run all of your.

538
00:35:26,640 --> 00:35:30,670
tests with certain features of your program turned off.

539
00:35:30,680 --> 00:35:32,710
That sounds counterintuitive, right?

540
00:35:32,710 --> 00:35:35,110
Why would you want to do that?

541
00:35:35,110 --> 00:35:39,190
Well, imagine a situation where, let's say you have some data structure,

542
00:35:39,200 --> 00:35:43,350
and you have two randomized operations that you can do on the data structure.

543
00:35:43,360 --> 00:35:47,270
One of them adds an element to it, and one takes an element out, right?

544
00:35:47,270 --> 00:35:50,070
If both of these things happen with 50% probability,

545
00:35:50,080 --> 00:35:53,350
the contents of the data structure will never get very large.

546
00:35:53,360 --> 00:35:58,350
You would require like, let's say you want to get a hundred things in the data structure.

547
00:35:58,350 --> 00:36:02,550
and then like some garbage collection routine or some compaction happens.

548
00:36:02,550 --> 00:36:04,590
That would require a one in two to the one hundred chance to happen.

549
00:36:04,590 --> 00:36:07,150
if you're randomly adding and removing things.

550
00:36:07,160 --> 00:36:12,550
But, if we just temporarily disable the remove function,

551
00:36:12,550 --> 00:36:14,470
then you're guaranteed to get a whole bunch of adds in a row.

552
00:36:14,480 --> 00:36:18,350
and then you're going to trigger that compaction routine, right?

553
00:36:18,350 --> 00:36:20,150
And so, this is like a very motivating example,

554
00:36:20,150 --> 00:36:28,670
and it it's like you want to do this with everything, including with your fault injection, right?

555
00:36:28,670 --> 00:36:31,350
One of the most common mistakes that in the early days,

556
00:36:31,350 --> 00:36:35,150
before we did this automatically, the customers would make.

557
00:36:35,150 --> 00:36:37,630
is they would say, "Ah, I want my code to be maximum powerful.

558
00:36:37,630 --> 00:36:40,150
so I want to turn on every single fault."

559
00:36:40,160 --> 00:36:45,750
And the problem is there are some faults that will hide bugs, right?

560
00:36:45,750 --> 00:36:50,550
Like imagine there's a bug where your program gets into some really bad state,

561
00:36:50,550 --> 00:36:53,070
but if you restart the program, the bug goes away.

562
00:36:53,080 --> 00:36:58,710
Well, now if we are doing a fault that occasionally kills nodes, that can hide that bug, right?

563
00:36:58,710 --> 00:37:06,070
And so what you actually want to do is sometimes run with killing nodes completely turned off, because that might save you from some bug.

564
00:37:06,080 --> 00:37:11,350
And so it's it's exactly the same with everything else and with what kinds of requests you generate.

565
00:37:11,350 --> 00:37:15,790
and every other kind of behavior that you want to provoke in the system.

566
00:37:15,790 --> 00:37:19,870
Now, it's still possible to get this wrong, it's still possible to overlook things.

567
00:37:19,880 --> 00:37:27,031
And so that's why we come back to like the the sometimes assertions, the logging, that kind of stuff.

568
00:37:27,031 --> 00:37:31,390
If you know the transaction rollbacks are an important situation,

569
00:37:31,390 --> 00:37:36,470
you should be adding, you know, a sometimes assertion or a log to your rollback function.

570
00:37:36,470 --> 00:37:42,070
and then we should be looking at every test run and saying, "Did we make rollbacks happen?"

571
00:37:42,070 --> 00:37:44,350
And if not, that should be a red test failure, right?

572
00:37:44,350 --> 00:37:45,910
It It could mean that your program has changed.

573
00:37:45,920 --> 00:37:50,670
Maybe you accidentally disabled the rollback function, and that's, you know, now we found a real bug.

574
00:37:50,670 --> 00:37:53,230
But it could also mean that the tests are weak, right?

575
00:37:53,230 --> 00:37:57,590
It could mean that for whatever reason we're not making transactions rollback enough,

576
00:37:57,590 --> 00:37:59,350
and that could be very, very important information.

577
00:37:59,360 --> 00:38:03,070
Mhm. Mhm. So, in that example of the transaction rollback,

578
00:38:03,070 --> 00:38:08,350
you know, the other trigger event was a leader election, right?

579
00:38:08,350 --> 00:38:14,070
So, again, how do you draw the correlation of what's happening at the lowest bits. and bytes level to at the more application level?

580
00:38:14,080 --> 00:38:17,110
How do you say that, hey, that actually is a leader election?

581
00:38:17,120 --> 00:38:19,390
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

582
00:38:19,390 --> 00:38:21,190
So, the easiest way to do this actually,

583
00:38:21,190 --> 00:38:26,631
so the way that requires the least investment from a user is you just look at lines of. code.

584
00:38:26,640 --> 00:38:29,510
Every line of code in your program has a purpose.

585
00:38:29,520 --> 00:38:32,311
Some of the lines of code are very boring. They run all the time, right?

586
00:38:32,320 --> 00:38:34,750
The code that like logs a string.

587
00:38:34,750 --> 00:38:38,390
That probably is running all the time in your program.

588
00:38:38,390 --> 00:38:43,110
And so we are going to see it running all the time. and we are quickly going to get bored of it.

589
00:38:43,120 --> 00:38:45,030
Across all the programs.

590
00:38:45,030 --> 00:38:47,710
Because the analytics will be an external event or external service.

591
00:38:47,720 --> 00:38:50,191
Totally. Totally true.

592
00:38:50,191 --> 00:38:52,990
But anything that runs rarely, that runs only sometimes,

593
00:38:53,000 --> 00:38:56,670
or that runs in very specific conditions, or that runs.

594
00:38:56,680 --> 00:38:59,830
You could also have, for example, a line of code.

595
00:38:59,840 --> 00:39:07,350
that very commonly runs together with some other line of code, but very, very rarely runs by itself.

596
00:39:07,350 --> 00:39:08,910
That could be something very interesting.

597
00:39:08,910 --> 00:39:13,990
So you're not just looking at which lines of code run or don't run at all.

598
00:39:13,990 --> 00:39:16,830
but you're actually also looking at the correlations between them.

599
00:39:16,830 --> 00:39:18,670
and when they run together with each other.

600
00:39:18,670 --> 00:39:20,950
or not and and what orders they run and so on.

601
00:39:20,950 --> 00:39:26,870
And this is a kind of analysis that human beings are very bad at doing.

602
00:39:26,870 --> 00:39:28,870
but it's a kind of analysis that AI is actually very good at doing,

603
00:39:28,880 --> 00:39:31,470
machine learning and so on.

604
00:39:31,470 --> 00:39:37,110
And so you can sort of look at the history of what the software has done.

605
00:39:37,120 --> 00:39:40,190
and then ask, is this novel?

606
00:39:40,190 --> 00:39:43,470
Like, is this interesting? Is this something This is very similar to what I've seen before.

607
00:39:43,480 --> 00:39:46,230
or is it very different from what I've seen before?

608
00:39:46,230 --> 00:39:49,070
And then you can use that as an input into where you want to continue testing.

609
00:39:49,080 --> 00:39:52,830
Mhm. So are you using AI ML here to do the correlation?

610
00:39:52,840 --> 00:39:55,991
Yeah. Yeah, yeah.

611
00:39:55,991 --> 00:39:58,590
We actually find It's It's funny.

