00:42:47,600 --> 00:42:53,710
which actually explicitly was able to assign error probabilities.

654
00:42:53,720 --> 00:42:58,470
How compatible would that be with what you're doing and how much would that alleviate that initial pain?

655
00:42:58,480 --> 00:43:00,430
I think that's a very interesting idea.

656
00:43:00,440 --> 00:43:06,550
So, one thing that we actually do encourage our customers to do.

657
00:43:06,550 --> 00:43:08,390
If you're If you're thinking about annotating,

658
00:43:08,390 --> 00:43:14,030
one thing that I think is a very, very powerful technique is something called bugification.

659
00:43:14,040 --> 00:43:20,190
And the idea of bugification is suppose you have some code which usually does one thing,

660
00:43:20,200 --> 00:43:24,790
but very rarely could do something else, right?

661
00:43:24,790 --> 00:43:28,150
For example, it could be like writing to a disk, right?

662
00:43:28,150 --> 00:43:30,510
Usually it writes all the data to the disk,

663
00:43:30,510 --> 00:43:32,190
but you know, as every systems programmer has learned the hard way.

664
00:43:32,200 --> 00:43:36,190
sometimes it writes some of the data to the disk.

665
00:43:36,190 --> 00:43:39,350
and gives you a special return code that says, "Hey, I only wrote some of the data."

666
00:43:39,350 --> 00:43:40,910
You know, by the way, net- networks work the same way, right?

667
00:43:40,920 --> 00:43:44,150
Well, well, usually flush and fsync are actually not on by default, so.

668
00:43:44,160 --> 00:43:46,151
Well, that's true, too. Yeah.

669
00:43:46,151 --> 00:43:49,750
Right, so Right, anytime you have something like this that usually does one thing,

670
00:43:49,750 --> 00:43:51,990
but very rarely does something else.

671
00:43:52,000 --> 00:43:56,670
